   "captions_sha256": "3d0484190457dcddf9dbc19cfb5e309d679a0ee015f187d100e275ed1b32a6cb",
   "created_at": "2026-11-22T03:19:03Z",
   "description": "How NASA moved the Roman Space Telescope: an 18,000-pound observatory sealed in a portable clean room, driven through Baltimore at night at about 2 mph, shipped south on NASA's Pegasus barge, and launched on a Falcon Heavy toward a point about a million miles from Earth.\n\nIn this episode:\n0:00 2 mph through Baltimore\n0:17 Roman and the CHARIOT\n0:50 The 13-hour night drive\n1:21 Pegasus and the Chesapeake Bay Bridge\n1:52 Arriving at Kennedy Space Center\n2:28 Launch to L2\n2:48 What Roman will see\n\nSources:\n- NASA, \"NASA's Next Generation Telescope Arrives in Florida Ahead of Launch\" (June 21, 2026): https://science.nasa.gov/blogs/roman/2026/06/21/nasas-next-generation-telescope-arrives-in-florida-ahead-of-launch/\n- NASA, Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope mission page: https://science.nasa.gov/mission/roman-space-telescope/\n- NASA SVS, Roman CHARIOT Transport B-roll: https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/15066\n- NASA SVS, Roman Being Loaded into the CHARIOT: https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/15068\n- NASA SVS, Roman Moving to Kennedy Space Center PHSF Building: https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/15071\n- NASA SVS, Roman Launches and a New Era Begins: https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/15089\n\nFootage: NASA's Goddard Space Flight Center and Kennedy Space Center via the Scientific Visualization Studio: https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/15064 , https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/15066 , https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/15067 (drone: Rob Fortunato and Christopher Albert), https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/15068 , https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/15071 , https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/15089 , https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/15000 , https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/15058 . Public domain; NASA does not endorse this video. Spacecraft-in-space shots and the journey map are NASA animations. Solar array shots are from an earlier test at Goddard. CHARIOT and L2 graphics: NEXORA.\nNarration: synthetic voice (ElevenLabs).\n\n#RomanSpaceTelescope #NASA #SpaceEngineering #NEXORA",
